Z||000000||Pilrulz||12-20-2001||01:22 PM||||all right, i have a duke in my garage on a stand, and we're going to start pulling the old one out(blew a hole in the #3 cyl) tomorrow.  so from anybody who has done it before, can you pull the engine through the top, or do you have to drop the cradle?  and, do you have any words of wisdom to share?<P>pil||||reg||12
Z||000001||K2m4||12-20-2001||01:35 PM||||it can be done either way! ive seen pics here on the forum of it done both ways. Ive never done it but i know it can be done the conventional way from the top!<P><P>------------------<BR>Kerm<BR>86 GT<BR>86 2M4||||reg||1
Z||000002||Elenor84||12-20-2001||04:40 PM||||Just drop the cradle...its so easy...don't muck around with taking it out the top...u might damage somethign...just drop the cradle then swap the engines...I took a v6 out in a matter of  hours...very easy..<P>------------------<BR>Lance<BR>Click Image to go to site.<BR><A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/_XMCM/lancegspage/Index.html" TARGET=_blank> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Side1.jpg"> </A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroJones||12-20-2001||08:09 PM||||On the 4'cyl's (Manual) It is actually easier to yank the motor out the top.  Just remove the AC compressor (leave it attached to the lines, just take it off the bracket, UNLESS there is no freon, then just unconnect the lines at the firewall) and also take off the Alternator.  Using, this, and a Cherrypicker, i was able to remove my 87 Duke motor in a lil under 2 hours with me and my friend.  The V6 on the other hand, is an entirelly diff beast.   If you CAN jack up the car far enough to drop the cradle, it is easier, but requires more tools, and the car is un-movable should the need arise.  Lots of options, be sure to pick the one best for you and your budget!<P>-KArl||||reg||1
